<td id="S2.T1.8.1.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.43</td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.11</td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.7.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.7.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">0.50</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.8.1.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">UNICT Solution <sub id="S2.T1.8.1.8.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#EE822F;">(2024 Top1)</span></sub></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">RGB+Eyes</td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.8.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">0.51</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.8.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">0.95</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.8.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.8.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.93</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.8.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.06</td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.8.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.09</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.8.1.9" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FDE8DA;">
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.9.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FDE8DA;">DR-MoE <sub id="S2.T1.8.1.9.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.9.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#EE822F;">(Ours)</span></sub></span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.9.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FDE8DA;">RGB</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.9.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FDE8DA;">0.57</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.9.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.9.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FDE8DA;">0.97</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.9.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.9.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FDE8DA;">0.60</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.9.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.9.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FDE8DA;">0.08</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.8.1.9.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.9.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FDE8DA;">0.63</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once the joint feature <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{\text{joint}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mtext>joint</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{\text{joint}}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained, it is passed through a classification module to determine whether the observed action contains a mistake. However, due to the diversity of video types and the varying degrees of class imbalance inherent in the HoloAssist datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, relying on a single classification head often fails to achieve satisfactory performance across all scenarios. To address this challenge, we construct three distinct classifiers, denoted as <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(3)}</annotation></semantics></math>, each trained using a different optimization strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reweight CE Loss.</span> The first classifier, <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, is trained using a reweighted cross-entropy (CE) loss that explicitly compensates for class imbalance. The loss function is defined as:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{WCE}=-\sum_{y}w_{y}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{[y=\hat{y}]}\log p_{y}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{WCE}=-\sum_{y}w_{y}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{[y=\hat{y}]}\log p_{y},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the ground-truth label, <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is the predicted label, <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is the predicted probability for class <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is the weight assigned to class <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, calculated as inversely proportional to its frequency in the training data, <span id="S2.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{y}=\frac{1}{f_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{y}=\frac{1}{f_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> being the class frequency. This weighting mechanism compels the classifier to pay greater attention to the tail classes, which in our context often correspond to subtle or infrequent error patterns. As a result, <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is particularly effective in improving recall for underrepresented mistake instances.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AUC Loss.</span> The second classifier, <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>, directly optimizes the Area Under the ROC Curve (AUC), which better reflects model performance in imbalanced classification settings. Specifically, the AUC loss is formulated as a pairwise ranking problem, aiming to assign higher prediction scores to positive (mistake) instances than to negative (correct) ones. The loss function is defined as:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{AUC}=\frac{1}{n^{+}n^{-}}\sum\limits_{i=1}^{n^{+}}\sum\limits_{j=1}^{n^{-}}\ell\left(f^{(2)}(\mathbf{F}_{\text{joint}}^{i,+})-f^{(2)}(\mathbf{F}_{\text{joint}}^{i,-})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐅</mi><mtext>joint</mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐅</mi><mtext>joint</mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{AUC}=\frac{1}{n^{+}n^{-}}\sum\limits_{i=1}^{n^{+}}\sum\limits_{j=1}^{n^{-}}\ell\left(f^{(2)}(\mathbf{F}_{\text{joint}}^{i,+})-f^{(2)}(\mathbf{F}_{\text{joint}}^{i,-})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the numbers of mistake and correct samples respectively, <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{\text{joint}}^{i,+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐅</mi><mtext>joint</mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{\text{joint}}^{i,+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{\text{joint}}^{j,-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐅</mi><mtext>joint</mtext><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{\text{joint}}^{j,-}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the joint features corresponding to the <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th mistake sample and the <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th correct sample. The function <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is typically a convex surrogate loss such as hinge or logistic loss. This formulation explicitly encourages the model to rank mistake segments above correct ones, thereby enhancing discriminative capability under long-tailed class distributions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LA Loss optimized with SAM.</span> The third classifier, <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(3)}</annotation></semantics></math>, leverages a combination of LA Loss and SAM to achieve improved generalization and robustness. Its objective is expressed as:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{LA}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ell\big((f^{(3)}(\mathbf{F}_{\text{joint}}^{i})+\log\bf{p}),y_{i}\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐅</mi><mtext>joint</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>𝐢</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{LA}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ell\big((f^{(3)}(\mathbf{F}_{\text{joint}}^{i})+\log\bf{p}),y_{i}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the cross-entropy loss, and <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bf{p}=[f_{1},\cdots,f_{C}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mn>𝟏</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>𝐂</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bf{p}=[f_{1},\cdots,f_{C}]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the class frequency vector. SAM further enhances this effect by seeking flat minima through an adversarial optimization scheme, formulated as:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\theta}\max_{\|\epsilon\|\leq\rho}\mathcal{L}(f^{(3)}_{\theta+\epsilon})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\theta}\max_{\|\epsilon\|\leq\rho}\mathcal{L}(f^{(3)}_{\theta+\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where the inner maximization identifies parameter perturbations within radius <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> that locally worsen the loss. This forces the model to converge to parameter regions with improved generalization properties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To unify the predictions from these heterogeneous classifiers, we introduce a Classification Mixture-of-Experts (C-MoE) module that assigns dynamic weights to the outputs of <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(3)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The final prediction is computed as:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=\sum_{k=1}^{3}\beta_{k}f^{(k)}(\mathbf{F}_{\text{joint}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mtext>joint</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\sum_{k=1}^{3}\beta_{k}f^{(k)}(\mathbf{F}_{\text{joint}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where each <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learnable expert weight reflecting the relevance of the <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th classifier’s decision for the current input. This adaptive fusion enables the model to selectively emphasize the most suitable decision path for each instance, ultimately improving robustness and accuracy in complex mistake detection scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe some details of the experiments and present our results.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct all experiments using eight NVIDIA A100 GPUs. For the feature extraction module, we adopt ViViT-B/<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> as our backbone and follow the original settings for hyperparameters. During fine-tuning, we apply LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> with a rank of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> to the attention layers, specifically targeting the query and value projection matrices. For the classification module, SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> is employed with a perturbation radius <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>. The entire model is optimized using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>. We set the batch size to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math> clips, each consisting of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math> frames. The model is trained for a total of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> epochs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.T1" title="In 2.3 Classification Mixture of Experts (C-MoE) ‣ 2 Method ‣ Dual-Stage Reweighted MoE for Long-Tailed Egocentric Mistake Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the performance of various models on the mistake detection task. Compared to Random and TimeSformer, our method significantly improves the F-score. Furthermore, in comparison to the top-performing method of 2024, our method achieves a substantial improvement in mistake recall. Most notably, our method attains competitive performance using only the RGB modality, matching or even surpassing models that rely on multimodal inputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
